{\"id\":\"V1\",\"title\":\"Conductivity-conditioned generation of disordered Li-halide solid electrolytes\",\"summary\":\"Battery crystal discovery, restricted to the disordered Li-halide solid electrolytes where fast Li conduction actually lives. Crystal foundation models: there's hella, but MatterGen's <=20-atom unit-cell limit and stable ordered-crystal training set do not cover the partially-occupied configurations that fast Li conductors need. Fine-tune MatterGen's adapter on predicted ionic conductivity using DTU's 400k-structure halide database as the domain-specific transport label source, generate and screen 1k-100k candidates per arm, NEB-validate the top ten, and compare hit rate against unconditioned generation and ICSD screening, all within the halide family. Compute axis: generated candidate crystal structures screened per arm, 1,000-100,000 candidates, 3 levels.\",\"root_link\":\"is the root question\",\"compute_open\":\"whether halide-domain fine-tuning shifts the hit-rate gap outside seed noise\",\"sequential\":\"not given (seed idea from notes)\"}
{\"id\":\"V2\",\"title\":\"Spend the compute on NEB-validated candidates, scored against measured conductivity\",\"summary\":\"Battery crystal discovery, with the compute spent on deeper validation rather than more generation. Fine-tune MatterGen's adapter on predicted log conductivity, generate candidates, then scale the number per arm that receive DFT-NEB migration-barrier validation from the top ten up to the top 100-1,000, comparing against unconditioned generation and ICSD screening. Score candidates by NEB barriers and predicted conductivity calibrated against the measured conductivity of PNNL's synthesized NaxLi3-xYCl6 family rather than predicted sigma alone. Compute axis: NEB-validated candidates per arm, 10-1,000 candidates, 3 levels.\",\"root_link\":\"is the root question\",\"compute_open\":\"whether the conditioned generator's ranking survives NEB validation against measured halide conductivity data\",\"sequential\":\"not given (seed idea from notes)\"}
{\"id\":\"V3\",\"title\":\"Benchmark conditioned generation against Azure Quantum Elements' 32-million-candidate screen\",\"summary\":\"Battery crystal discovery, judged against the front-end Microsoft actually uses. Fine-tune MatterGen's adapter on predicted ionic conductivity, generate and screen 1k-100k candidates per arm, NEB-validate the top ten, and compare hit rate not against ICSD screening but against Azure Quantum Elements' 32-million enumerated-candidate screen. The question becomes whether conductivity-conditioned generation beats enumeration on hit rate per candidate proposed. Compute axis: generated candidate crystal structures screened per arm, 1,000-100,000 candidates, 3 levels.\",\"root_link\":\"is the root question\",\"compute_open\":\"whether conditioning beats the 32-million enumeration screen only at the 100,000-candidate level\",\"sequential\":\"not given (seed idea from notes)\"}

